Accurate multipath channel estimation is fundamental to underwater acoustic communications, radar, and localization. The Space-Alternating Generalized Expectation-Maximization (SAGE) algorithm is widely used for iterative multipath decomposition, yet its M-step is conventionally restricted to an integer sampling grid, incurring a structural grid-mismatch error that cannot be reduced by additional EM iterations.

We propose Continuous-Delay SAGE (CD-SAGE), a two-stage fractional delay refinement strategy that augments the SAGE M-step with three-point parabolic interpolation for coarse sub-sample delay estimation at $\bigO(1)$ cost, followed by local grid refinement on a narrow window. The combined approach achieves continuous-valued delay estimation without constructing a fine-grid dictionary.

We establish a rigorous theoretical foundation: Lemma~1 proves the $\delta$-invariance of the parabolic denominator under a quadratic autocorrelation model, guaranteeing exact noiseless recovery; Theorem~1 derives the closed-form asymptotic variance; and Corollary~1 proves that the variance-to-CRB ratio is independent of SNR. Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate that CD-SAGE reduces delay estimation error by two orders of magnitude compared to integer-grid SAGE and attains near-CRB performance. Per-path analysis reveals that continuous-delay estimation eliminates the quantization floor on isolated paths, while iterative E-step interference cancellation resolves closely-spaced paths inaccessible to dictionary methods. Validation on the Watermark benchmark supports these advantages on real measured multipath channels.
